  "title": "Read replicas: the bug factory you built",
  "content": "read replicas has a habit of breaking in ways the monitoring wasn't watching for. The gap between the demo and the incident report is where this actually lives. Here's the fuller picture, with the tradeoffs left in.\n\nRead replicas feel like free capacity. You add one, point your read traffic at it, and dashboards go green. Then, weeks later, a user reports that their profile picture reverted to the old one, or that an order they just placed is missing from the order history. The replica didn't fail — it did exactly what it was designed to do. The bug is in the assumption that a replica is a read-only copy of the primary at all times. It isn't. It's a copy that lags, sometimes by milliseconds, sometimes by minutes, and that lag is invisible to your application code until it isn't.\n\nThe problem this post solves: how to find and fix the read-after-write inconsistencies that read replicas introduce before your users do.\n\n## The gap between what the docs say and what production needs\n\nOfficial documentation for managed databases typically describes replication as asynchronous and notes that replicas may lag. That's accurate and almost useless for application design. The docs tell you the mechanism; they don't tell you which of your queries can tolerate stale data and which cannot. That classification is an application-level decision, and most teams never make it explicitly. Instead, they route reads to replicas by default — often at the ORM, connection-pooler, or proxy layer — and hope.\n\nThe counterintuitive part is that the bugs caused by stale reads rarely look like data-freshness bugs. They look like authorization failures, duplicate submissions, phantom 404s, and \"impossible\" state transitions. A user updates their email, then immediately tries to log in with the new address and gets \"invalid credentials\" because the auth service read from a replica that hadn't caught up. A payment webhook reads the order from a replica, doesn't see the status change yet, and reprocesses the charge. These are not replica failures. They are read-after-write violations, and they are endemic to any system that separates writes from reads without a consistency plan.\n\n## How read replicas actually work under the hood\n\nA read replica is a second database instance that receives a stream of changes from the primary. In PostgreSQL, that stream is WAL (write-ahead log) records shipped to the replica, which replays them. In MySQL, it's the binary log. Managed services like Amazon RDS, Aurora, and Google Cloud SQL all use variants of this model. The key property is that replication is asynchronous by default: the primary commits a transaction and acknowledges the client before the replica has applied it. The replica applies changes as fast as it can, but there is no bound on the delay.\n\nThat delay is called replication lag. It can be near-zero on an idle system and spike under load. A long-running transaction on the primary, a bulk load, a network hiccup, or a replica that falls behind on IO can all push lag from milliseconds to minutes. The replica is still healthy by most monitoring definitions — it's up, it's connected, it's applying WAL — but it is not caught up.\n\nCrucially, the primary has no idea which replica has applied which transaction unless you ask it. PostgreSQL exposes this through `pg_current_wal_lsn()` on the primary and `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()` on the replica; the difference is the replay lag in bytes. MySQL exposes `SHOW REPLICA STATUS` with `Seconds_Behind_Source`. These are the numbers you need, and they are almost never wired into application logic.\n\n## Step-by-step implementation with real code\n\nThe fix is not to abandon replicas. It's to make the read path aware of what it needs. There are three practical strategies, and most systems need a mix.\n\n### 1. Route reads that follow a write back to the primary\n\nThis is the simplest and most effective fix. If a request writes and then reads the same entity, that read must go to the primary — or at least to a replica known to have applied the write. In practice, teams implement this with a \"sticky primary\" window: after a write, the same session or request context routes reads to the primary for a short period. The window is a heuristic, not a guarantee, but it eliminates the most common user-facing inconsistency.\n\nA minimal implementation in Python with SQLAlchemy:\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import sessionmaker\n\nprimary_engine = create_engine(\"postgresql://app@primary/db\")\nreplica_engine = create_engine(\"postgresql://app@replica/db\")\nPrimarySession = sessionmaker(bind=primary_engine)\nReplicaSession = sessionmaker(bind=replica_engine)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef read_session(after_write: bool = False):\n    # after_write is set by the request handler when the same request\n    # performed a write to the entity being read.\n    factory = PrimarySession if after_write else ReplicaSession\n    session = factory()\n    try:\n        yield session\n    finally:\n        session.close()\n```\n\nThe hard part is not the code — it's threading `after_write` through your request handlers. That requires knowing which reads are causally dependent on which writes. A common pattern is to set a flag on the request context when any write occurs and have the data-access layer consult it. This is coarse (it sends all reads in that request to the primary) but correct.\n\n### 2. Wait for the replica to catch up to a known LSN\n\nWhen you need to read from a replica but must see a specific write, you can capture the primary's WAL position at write time and wait for the replica to reach it. PostgreSQL makes this possible with `pg_current_wal_lsn()` and `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()`. The write path records the LSN; the read path polls the replica until its replay LSN is at least that value, with a timeout.\n\n```sql\n-- On the primary, immediately after the write transaction commits:\nSELECT pg_current_wal_lsn();\n\n-- On the replica, before serving the read:\nSELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= '0/16B6C50'::pg_lsn;\n```\n\nThis is precise but adds latency: you're waiting for replication, which is exactly the delay you were trying to avoid. Use it for reads where correctness matters more than a few milliseconds — for example, reading back a just-created resource in an API response.\n\n### 3. Make the read idempotent and tolerate staleness\n\nSome reads genuinely don't need freshness. A product listing, a public profile, a feed of posts — these can be seconds stale without anyone noticing. The mistake is treating all reads the same. Classify them. Reads that feed into a write decision (e.g., checking a balance before a debit) must be fresh. Reads that are purely presentational can be stale. Document the classification in code, not in a wiki that nobody reads.\n\n## How to measure performance, and what to expect\n\nReplication lag is the metric that matters, and it should be monitored per replica, not in aggregate. On PostgreSQL, a query like this on the replica gives you the replay delay in seconds:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replication_delay;\n```\n\nOn a healthy, lightly loaded system this is typically sub-second. Under bulk writes or network pressure it can grow to tens of seconds. The number itself is not the problem; the problem is that application code has no visibility into it. If your read path doesn't know the current lag, it can't decide whether a stale read is acceptable.\n\nA useful exercise: instrument your read endpoints with a counter for \"read served from replica where the client had written to the same entity within the last N seconds.\" That counter is a direct measure of your exposure to read-after-write bugs. It should be zero for critical paths. If it isn't, you've found the bug before a user did.\n\n## The failure modes nobody warns you about\n\nThe obvious failure mode is a user not seeing their own write. The less obvious ones are worse because they corrupt state rather than just confuse a user.\n\n**Duplicate side effects.** A worker reads a job from a replica, sees status `pending`, and starts processing. The primary had already marked it `completed` a moment earlier, but the replica hadn't caught up. The worker processes it again. If the job isn't idempotent, you've double-charged a customer or sent a duplicate email. This is a read-after-write violation in a background process, and it's common because workers are often pointed at replicas to offload the primary.\n\n**Authorization bypass.** A user's role is revoked in the primary. A request hits a replica that still shows the old role and grants access. The window is small, but for sensitive operations it's a real security gap. Permission checks are reads, and they are almost always routed to replicas.\n\n**Phantom 404s.** A resource is created, and the client immediately fetches it by ID. The read goes to a replica that hasn't seen the insert, and the API returns 404. The client may retry, may show an error, or may cache the 404. From the user's perspective, the create succeeded but the resource doesn't exist.\n\n**Broken pagination.** A list endpoint reads from a replica while writes are happening on the primary. Pages can skip or duplicate items because the underlying data changed between requests. This is a consistency problem even without replicas, but replicas widen the window.\n\n**Silent data loss in read-modify-write cycles.** An application reads an entity from a replica, modifies it, and writes it back to the primary. If the replica was stale, the write overwrites newer data with older data. This is the most dangerous failure mode because it's a lost update that no error surfaces.\n\n## Tools and libraries worth your time\n\nYou don't need a custom proxy to solve this, but you do need a layer that understands read/write intent. A few categories of tooling are worth evaluating:\n\n- **Connection poolers with routing rules.** PgBouncer and ProxySQL can route queries based on statement type, but they generally can't know whether a read follows a write in the same logical operation. They're useful for load balancing, not for consistency.\n- **ORM-level read/write splitting.** Some ORMs and database toolkits support a primary/replica configuration with a \"sticky\" option that routes reads to the primary after a write within the same transaction or session. The quality varies; check whether the stickiness is per-transaction or per-request, and whether it covers reads in separate requests.\n- **Managed database proxies.** Amazon RDS Proxy and similar services can route to replicas, but they don't solve read-after-write consistency for you; they just make the routing configurable. Read the documentation carefully on what guarantees they do and don't provide.\n- **Application-level session stores.** If you already have a session or request context, storing the last-write timestamp or LSN there is a lightweight way to make routing decisions without new infrastructure.\n\nThe tooling matters less than the discipline of classifying reads. A team that knows which reads are freshness-sensitive can implement routing with a few lines of code; a team that doesn't will struggle regardless of the proxy.\n\n## When this approach is the wrong choice\n\nRead replicas are not always the answer, and adding consistency logic is not always worth it. If your workload is write-heavy and read-light, a replica may add cost and complexity without relieving much pressure. If your application is already tolerant of stale reads by design — for example, an analytics dashboard that refreshes every few minutes — the consistency work is unnecessary. And if your primary is not actually saturated, adding a replica may just move the bottleneck to replication lag without improving throughput.\n\nThe more interesting question is whether you need a replica at all. Vertical scaling of the primary, query optimization, and caching can often absorb read load without introducing a second source of truth. A replica is a tool for scaling reads and for failover, not a default architecture. Teams that add one because it's the standard pattern often inherit the consistency bugs without needing the capacity.\n\n## Common production pitfalls and what they cost\n\n**Pitfall: assuming lag is always milliseconds.** It usually is, until it isn't. A bulk migration or a long transaction can push lag to minutes. If your application has no timeout on \"wait for replica,\" it can hang. Always bound the wait and fall back to the primary.\n\n**Pitfall: monitoring replica health, not replica freshness.** A replica that is up but an hour behind is healthy by uptime checks and useless for reads. Alert on lag, not just availability.\n\n**Pitfall: testing against a single database.** Integration tests that run against one instance never exercise the read-after-write path. If you can't run a replica in CI, at least write a test that simulates stale reads by injecting a delay or a mock that returns old data.\n\n**Pitfall: forgetting background workers.** Cron jobs and queue consumers are often pointed at replicas to reduce primary load. They are also the most likely to perform read-modify-write cycles. Audit them separately.\n\n**Pitfall: believing the ORM handles it.** Some ORMs advertise automatic read/write splitting. Read the fine print: many route based on whether a transaction is open, not on whether the specific entity was just written. A read in a new transaction after a write in a previous one may still go to the replica.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do I know if my application has read-after-write bugs?**\nInstrument reads that occur shortly after writes to the same entity and count how many are served by a replica. If the count is non-zero for user-facing flows, you have exposure. You can also add assertions in staging that compare replica and primary results for critical reads.\n\n**Can I just set synchronous replication and be done?**\nSynchronous replication ensures the primary waits for at least one replica to acknowledge each commit before returning. That eliminates lag for that replica, but it adds write latency and reduces availability if the replica is slow or unreachable. It's a valid choice for some systems, but it doesn't solve the routing problem: you still need to send the read to the replica that has the data.\n\n**Why does my ORM's read/write splitting not fix this?**\nMost ORM splitting is based on transaction boundaries or explicit hints. It cannot infer that a read in a new request depends on a write from a previous request. Consistency is an application concern, not a driver concern.\n\n**What is a safe replication lag threshold?**\nThere is no universal number. The threshold should be derived from your application's tolerance for stale reads. For a read that must reflect a write from the same user session, the tolerance is effectively zero, so route to the primary. For presentational reads, a lag of several seconds may be fine. Measure the lag and set alerts relative to the reads that matter.\n\n## What to do next\n\nOpen your data-access layer and find every place a read is routed to a replica. For each one, ask: does this read ever need to reflect a write from the same user or process within the last few seconds? If the answer is yes, route it to the primary or implement a wait-for-LSN check. Start with the read that follows a user profile update — that's the one most likely to be reported as a bug. Add a counter for replica reads that follow a write in the same request context, and watch it for a day. That counter is your early warning system, and it takes less than thirty minutes to add.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
